<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Need Two Papayas, Please.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist peers in following multi-step school procedures. Concept: Explain the steps to solve a problem using practical examples.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers in following multi-step school procedures by acting out steps (e.g., mimes "Next, pay for your fruit."). Can guide peers through solving simple problems by modeling steps, using clarifying questions, and providing feedback (e.g., "First, choose fruit." and points to a fruit picture, then asks, "Which fruit do you want?" and provides feedback, "That's a great choice. What's next?"). Can summarize instructions for peers, using visuals or simple keywords (e.g., points to each item on a checklist and says, "Step one, step two…").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #e0e6ea;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</b></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</b></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays flashcards of farm stand vocabulary (e.g., <i>mango, pineapple, papaya, banana, tomato, lettuce</i>). Students chant the names of the fruits and vegetables. The teacher points to a fruit and asks, "What is this? Do you like it?" to activate prior knowledge.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to explain a simple procedure: "How to peel a banana." The teacher holds a banana and says, "First, take the banana. Next, peel the banana. Then, eat the banana. Delicious!" The teacher uses clear gestures and mimes the actions, emphasizing sequence words. The teacher also models polite requests: "Can you help me, please?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the modeled procedure and mediation words.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we 'eat' the banana first, or 'peel' it first?" (Expected answer: Peel it first!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word do I use for the first step?" (Expected answer: First!)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated step-by-step instructions (a short visual guide with text) titled "How to Buy Fruit at the Farm Stand." The guide includes simple sentences and pictures:
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 1: Choose your fruit.</b> (Picture of fruits: <i>papaya, mango, pineapple</i>)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 2: Ask the price.</b> (Picture of cashier, speech bubble: "How much is this papaya?")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 3: Pay the cashier.</b> (Picture of giving money)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Step 4: Get your fruit.</b> (Picture of taking a bag of fruit)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explain the steps to solve a problem using practical examples (e.g., how to buy fruit).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students match the instruction sentences to the correct picture (e.g., "Choose your fruit" with the picture of fruits).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks gist questions: "What is the first thing you do at the farm stand?" "Who do you pay?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice explaining steps to peers, using polite requests ("Can you help me, please?"), WH-questions ("How much is...?"), and "some/any" (e.g., "Do you have any apples?"). Focus on vocabulary: <i>papaya, banana, fruit, cashier, price, need, buy, ask, choose, want</i>. Pronunciation practice for /p/ in "papaya" and /b/ in "banana", and segmenting syllables (e.g., "ba-na-na").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice: Sequencing and Explaining.</b> Students receive a worksheet with jumbled steps for "How to make a fruit salad" (e.g., "Cut the fruit.", "Wash the fruit.", "Eat the fruit.", "Put fruit in a bowl."). In pairs, they order the steps (First, Next, Then, Finally) and one student explains the sequence to the other using the mediation language. The teacher guides them to use phrases like "First, you need to..." or "Next, you should..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice: Guiding with Questions.</b> In pairs, Student A describes an action (e.g., "I want to buy some mangoes."). Student B acts as a "helper" or "mediator." Student B asks clarifying questions and guides Student A through the steps from the "How to Buy Fruit" guide, modeling and providing feedback: "First, choose fruit. Which fruit do you want?" (Points to a mango picture). "That's a great choice. What's next?" (Prompt for "Ask the price").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/Peer Check: Summarizing Instructions.</b> Students are given simple scenarios (e.g., "Your friend doesn't know how to ask for the price." or "Your friend doesn't know what to do after choosing fruit."). In pairs, one student summarizes the relevant instruction using visuals (pointing to a picture) or keywords (e.g., "Step one, choose!"). The other student provides feedback on clarity. Teacher circulates, providing support with pronunciation of /p/ and /b/, and syllable segmentation for words like "pa-pa-ya" and "ba-na-na."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Local Farm Stand".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Role-Play (3-4 students per group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: Farm Stand Role-Play (Project 1).</b> Students work in small groups to simulate a farm stand transaction. Each group assigns roles: "Customer," "Seller," and "Helper/Mediator." Groups use realia or flashcards for farm stand items (<i>mango, pineapple, guava, papaya, banana, tomato, pepper, lettuce, egg, cheese, bread, milk</i>).</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The <b>Customer</b> must buy at least three items, using polite requests ("I need two papayas, please." "Can I have some milk?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The <b>Seller</b> responds to questions, states prices ("It's two dollars."), and asks about quantities ("How many papayas do you need?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The <b>Helper/Mediator</b> observes the interaction. If the Customer or Seller struggles to remember a step or a phrase (e.g., asking for the price, politely saying they don't have an item), the Mediator intervenes to assist. The Mediator can:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Act out the step (e.g., mime "Next, pay for your fruit.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Guide a peer by modeling a phrase (e.g., "Say, 'How much is this?'").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Summarize instructions using simple keywords or pointing to the visual guide.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ask a clarifying question (e.g., "Do you have any papayas?").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in using transactional language and polite requests, collaborative interaction, and effective peer assistance (mediation) to complete the transaction.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and Peer Assessment Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group performs their "Farm Stand Role-Play." The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess students' participation and the Mediator's effectiveness. Peers use a simple checklist to evaluate their group's Mediator.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Mediator's Role):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /p/ and /b/ words (e.g., <i>papaya, banana, pineapple</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>cashier, price, choose, buy</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanations/summaries when assisting peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of polite requests ("Can you help me, please?") and WH-questions ("How much is...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successful assistance in guiding peers through the transaction steps.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of "some" and "any" (e.g., "We have some apples," "Do you have any milk?").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I do well when I helped my friends today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word or phrase I learned to help someone is: __________"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How easy or difficult was it to help your friends understand the steps of buying fruit?" (Students can show thumbs up/down or use a simple happy/sad face.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one thing you learned about helping others speak English today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in facilitating mediation skills among students? (e.g., "The visual step-by-step guide was very effective for mediators to reference.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with explaining or clarifying? (e.g., "Some students need more practice with sentence frames for giving instructions. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence starters for the mediator role.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "Local Farm Stand" theme and project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly focusing on "My Family's Favorite Foods" or "Going to a Restaurant," building on food vocabulary and transactional language in a new context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>